The accelerated expansion of the universe [[1]-[5]] points to a dominant component known as dark energy (DE) [([6]-[16]]). Since its proposal by [17], [18], the $\Lambda$CDM model, featuring a cosmological constant $\Lambda$, has remained the standard paradigm due to its strong alignment with observations [([19]-([24])}]. In this context, $\Lambda$ represents a constant energy density with an equation of state (EoS) given by $w_{\Lambda}=-1$. However, the fundamental nature of DE remains uncertain [([25]-([30])}] because $w_{\Lambda}=-1$ implies zero pressure and entropy, contradicting the high-entropy, pressure-rich properties of the cosmological vacuum [([31]-([34])}] or the quantum zero-point energy [([35]-([38])}] inherent to space [([39]-([42])}] [see also ([43]-([

 natural deduction, coexist alongside well-established imperative algorithms expressed through pseudocode. The relationship between these two concepts can be defined by the equation: imperative algorithms equal proofs with cuts. This insight motivates a broader generalization of pseudocode, termed *logical pseudocodes*, which parallels natural deduction proofs within computability logic \cite{Jap03,Jap08}. In this system, every statement maps directly to a specific step in a natural deduction proof. Consequently, the primary advantage of logical pseudocodes over traditional pseudocode is the assurance that each statement adheres to correctness and safety relative to the initial specifications. Furthermore, this approach represents an extension of the computability logic web (\colw) that incorporates forward reasoning capabilities. The dialogue within the \colw between a human author and the system is enhanced by allowing the author to assert claims using logical pseudocodes instead of standard pseudocode. The system then automatically verifies these claims against the natural deduction proofs associated with the specifications, either constructing the proof autonomously or assisting the author in doing so with minimal effort. Ultimately, this results in a direct translation into logical pseudocodes that the author can utilize with confidence. This paper outlines the specification of this system, its implementation, and provides examples demonstrating the equivalence of logical pseudocodes and natural deduction proofs via the \colw. Keywords: logical pseudocode, computability logic, proofs, algorithms, forward reasoning, CLiQ. Classification: F.3.1, H.1.0, I.2.

Introduction {#introduction}

